{:edb/db-before-t 2282 :edb/db-after-t 2283 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535595 50 #inst "2026-10-14T17:31:56.558Z" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186054800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521563 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521514 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061388 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520967 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061388 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520967 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061898 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061899 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061900 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061901 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061902 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520905 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520988 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520990 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521075 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521104 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186061897 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521075 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062420 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062421 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062422 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062423 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062424 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520848 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521061 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521100 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521221 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521323 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186062419 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521323 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064028 1071 "An $8\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost is casting a shadow of a $2\\,\\text{m}$ man onto the ground. The man walks away from the lamppost with a speed of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$. How fast is the length of the man's shadow changing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064028 1071 "An $8\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost is casting a shadow of a $2\\,\\text{m}$ man onto the ground. The man walks away from the lamppost with a speed of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the man's shadow changing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064028 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/29/29ef832326fff9e5b274c92ae51f2f32a88d463980fd4c109773086c344a76bb.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the man's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's velocity is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp, and therefore, the distance between him and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe need to find a relation between $x$ and $y$. Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x + y}{8} &= \\frac{y}{2} \\\\\n2x + 2y &= 8y \\\\\n2x &= 6y \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} =2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (2.4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\\n&= 0.8m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the man's shadow increases at a rate of $0.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064028 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/29/29ef832326fff9e5b274c92ae51f2f32a88d463980fd4c109773086c344a76bb.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the man's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's velocity is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp, and therefore, the distance between him and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ 2x+2y & =8y \\\\ 2x & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} =2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the man's shadow increases at a rate of $0.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064135 1071 "A $6\\,\\text{m}$ tall street lamp is casting a shadow of a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ woman onto the ground. The woman is walking towards the lamppost with a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{m/s}$. How fast is the length of the woman's shadow decreasing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064135 1071 "A $6\\,\\text{m}$ tall street lamp is casting a shadow of a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ woman onto the ground. The woman is walking towards the lamppost with a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the woman's shadow decreasing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064135 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0b/0bdd4280035b896f113e8d7a27599e488958dae40235faad1658e85710465211.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the woman's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the woman's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nNotice that the woman's velocity is *negative* because she is walking *toward* the lamp, and therefore the distance between her and the lamp *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe need to find a relation between $x$ and $y$. Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x + y}{6} &= \\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\\n1.5x + 1.5y &= 6y \\\\\n1.5x &= 4.5y \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\ninto the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac13 \\cdot (-1.5) = -0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the woman's shadow is decreasing at $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064135 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0b/0bdd4280035b896f113e8d7a27599e488958dae40235faad1658e85710465211.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the woman's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the woman's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the woman's velocity is *negative* because she is walking *toward* the lamp, and therefore the distance between her and the lamp *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =6y \\\\ 1.5x & =4.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac13 \\cdot (-1.5) = -0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the woman's shadow is decreasing at $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521501 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521452 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521468 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521499 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521526 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521531 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064364 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521524 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521542 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521561 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521503 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521508 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521533 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521480 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521530 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521546 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521558 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521454 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521457 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521497 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521456 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064379 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521552 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521513 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521459 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521462 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521474 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521481 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521490 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521458 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521472 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521477 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521523 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064436 1050 1140.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064453 1050 720.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064528 1071 "$206 - 364 =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064528 1071 "$206-364=${{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064528 1074 "Since $364$ is larger than $206,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n364-206\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,14\\end{aligned} \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n364-206=158.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n206-364=-158.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064640 1071 "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3\\text{ mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12\\text{ mm}?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064640 1071 "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3mm/s$. At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12mm$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064640 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2(12)=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $72\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064727 1071 "A $60\\,\\text{in}$ tall runner is training at night in a park. While running past a lamppost that is $100\\,\\text{in}$ tall, she notices her shadow is growing. Her watch tells her that she is travelling at $50\\,\\text{in/s}.$ How fast is the length of her shadow changing while she runs away from the lamppost?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186064727 1071 "A $60in$ tall runner is training at night in a park. While running past a lamppost that is $100in$ tall, she notices her shadow is growing. Her watch tells her that she is travelling at $50in/s$. How fast is the length of her shadow changing while she runs away from the lamppost?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186064727 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4ef1ba4975846a5375a18dee6cb4a55ba84d22d7c98b19eeaeebbb6b0017b623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the runner's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the person's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the runner's velocity is positive because she is running away from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the runner and the lamp increases with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{100} & =\\frac{y}{60} \\\\ 60x+60y & =100y \\\\ 60x & =40y \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (50) \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{2} \\\\  & =75\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the runner's shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{in/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186065386 1064 "Suppose that Bob, who is ![](images/ae/ae1e193fbd577e9009d2601d3f65608af0827a16092c4046543cdb1ccddb9f74.svg) 