417 true] [17592186082511 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520932 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082904 1071 "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\n \n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082904 1071 "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082904 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x)$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(4)) &= f^{-1}(-1) \\\\\n4 &= f^{-1}(-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082904 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(4)) & ={f}^{-1}(-1) \\\\ 4 & ={f}^{-1}(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082905 1071 "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082905 1071 "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082905 1074 "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(-4)) &= f^{-1}(3) \\\\\n- 4 &= f^{-1}(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(3) = -4\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082905 1074 "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-4)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ -4 & ={f}^{-1}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(3) = -4.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082906 1071 "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082906 1071 "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082906 1074 "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(2)) &= f^{-1}(-3) \\\\\n2 &= f^{-1}(-3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-3) = 2\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082906 1074 "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-3) = 2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082907 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082907 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186082907 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 4x - 2k \\\\\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k \\\\\n&= 4(\\frac{x}{4} - 2) - 2k \\\\\n&= x - 8 - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f$, we also know that\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nEquating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\n- 8 - 2k &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= -8 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186082907 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x.\n$$\n\nTo find $g(