3+9x^2+6x-6+\\frac{4}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by the original\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nfactor to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x^{4} + 7x^{3} - 10x + 8}{2 - 3x} &= -\\frac{1}{3}(3x^{3} + 9x^{2} + 6x - 6 + (4)/((x - \\frac{2}{3}))) \\\\\n&=-x^{3} - 3x^{2} - 2x + 2 + \\frac{4}{2 - 3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083005 1074 "First, we write the divisor in standard form as $-3x+2.$ Then, we factor out the leading coefficient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3x+2} & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\dfrac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)},$ remembering to include a coefficient of zero for the $x^2$ term, we arrive at the following:\n\n![](images/7a/7a44598088833fa475191b39579bb82935074ae5c470e5a2139555818b91af08.svg)\n\nReading off the bottom row, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)} = 3x^3+9x^2+6x-6+\\frac{4}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by the original $-\\dfrac 1 3$ factor to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{2-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}(3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+6x-6+\\frac{4}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-2x+2+\\frac{4}{2-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083017 1074 "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120.\n$$\n\n- Computing $2!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n2! = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5!\\,2! & =5!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =120\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083017 1074 "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n-