d}\\frac{5!}{0!} & =\\frac{120}{1}=120\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083137 1074 "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5$! using the definition, we get\n$5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120$.\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n$0! = 1$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5!}{0!} &= \\frac{120}{1} = 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521500 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521525 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521511 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521502 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083218 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521489 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083218 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521496 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521536 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521506 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083357 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521507 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083357 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521521 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521568 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083359 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521543 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083359 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521571 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083423 1050 780.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083443 1050 480.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083446 1050 420.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083457 1050 420.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083597 1071 "$\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083597 1071 "What is $\\frac{18x^{2} + 27x}{6x - 1}$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083597 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6(x-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $18$ | $27$ | $0$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n|  | $18$ | $30$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})} & =\\frac{1}{6}(18x+30+\\frac{5}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}) \\\\  & =3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083662 1071 "Suppose that $f(x) = \\frac{x}{3} + 7$ and $g(x) = 3x + k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then $k =$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083662 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ and $g(x)=3x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083662 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace everxy occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k \\\\  & =3(\\frac{x}{3}+7)+k \\\\  & =x+21+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+21+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+21+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 21+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083663 1071 "![](images/c9/c9360c7c07ecc0d78bc57fbcb56cc5ca3b799109ee48747bd4f651ad1209cd63.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A, B, C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(1)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083663 1071 "![](images/c9/c9360c7c07ecc0d78bc57fbcb56cc5ca3b799109ee48747bd4f651ad1209cd63.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083663 1074 "The point $C$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,1).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083666 1071 "![](images/46/46022df1ffb1b45ffe344658075dd363f3e2856798050fdbe85aacff0bc98ebe.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083666 1071 "![](images/46/46022df1ffb1b45ffe344658075dd363f3e2856798050fdbe85aacff0bc98ebe.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083666 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53ba6c80f60bdf35052e0bc018317f741d4ddd781ed9156f915df91b855a93f7.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1) & ⟶(1,0) \\\\ (-2,3) & ⟶(3,-2) \\\\ (-3,5) & ⟶(5,-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083667 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on the graph $y = f(x)$, which point must lie on the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083667 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083667 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-3,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083822 1071 "$\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083822 1071 "What is $\\frac{4x^{3} + 2x^{2} - x + 1}{x - 5}$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083822 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $4$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $20$ | $110$ | $545$ |\n|  | $4$ | $22$ | $109$ | $546$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}=4{x}^{2}+22x+109+\\frac{546}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083878 1071 "![](images/8c/8c884e34e0fd8b310984c2197ff0ea606033d00d63a0c6e0e04f0c993f519755.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(4)$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083878 1071 "![](images/8c/8c884e34e0fd8b310984c2197ff0ea606033d00d63a0c6e0e04f0c993f519755.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(4)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083878 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/a0/a0194a90466532c723c904d5ef9fea394e976b8b1f5287f066d5843f575e762e.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(5)=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(5)) & ={f}^{-1}(4) \\\\ 5 & ={f}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083926 1071 "$2!\\,3!=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083926 1071 "What is $2!3!$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083926 1074 "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $2!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n2!=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3!=3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2!\\,3! & =2!\\cdot 3! \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083930 1071 "$\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186083930 1071 "What is $\\frac{2x^{2} - 3x - 4}{x - 3}$?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186083930 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}=2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084421 1064 "Consider the function\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f(x)} = 4x-2\n$$\n\ndefined over the entire real line, and its inverse function\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f^{-1}(x)} = \\dfrac{1}{4}(x+2)\n$$\n\nIf we plot both\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\non the same diagram, we can see that the graph of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\nis a **reflection** of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nin the line $y=x$.\n\n![](images/ec/ec501e8c71fdcc1cfdd78e47ed81542bbba44aa8ac50084cc6ddfeb7eff011ff.png)\n\nEach point $(a,b)$ on the graph of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nhas a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$\n\nThat is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nFor example, notice that the point $A(2,6)$ on\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}y=f(x)}\n$$\n\nhas the corresponding point $A'(6,2)$ on\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084421 1064 "Consider the function \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f(x)} = 4x-2,\n$$\n\ndefined over the entire real line, and its inverse function \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f^{-1}(x)} = \\dfrac{1}{4}(x+2).\n$$\n\nIf we plot both ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}$ on the same diagram, we can see that the graph of ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}$ is a **reflection** of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ in the line $y=x.$ \n\n![](images/ec/ec501e8c71fdcc1cfdd78e47ed81542bbba44aa8ac50084cc6ddfeb7eff011ff.png)\n\nEach point $(a,b)$ on the graph of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$ That is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nFor example, notice that the point $A(2,6)$ on ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has the corresponding point $A'(6,2)$ on ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084439 1064 "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c)$. Here's how we do it.\n\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11$ | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}$. Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; ↓; 2$ | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}$. |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; 6; 2$ | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3}$. This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}$. Place the result below the next term. |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; 6; 2 \\mid 1$ | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}$. |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; 6\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}1$ | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}$. |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; 6\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid - 4$ | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}$. |\n| $3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 & -7\\end{vmatrix}11; 6\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}- 4 \\mid - 1$ | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}$. Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}}$, which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2$, $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case.\n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084439 1064 "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ![](images/86/8657b84c4cee9d122c5ec7fb62e652133c02b433e9bb73fb690573dd29857fe6.svg) | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/89/89ff1cd5f573ae387a716a8a446cd0867dfd464df0d9d9d91d4e9fd1f17f31ca.svg) | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ![](images/80/80e8e2f6092444058ec459f28518d0bd66b47858b9605562f8041aa09acda45e.svg) | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ![](images/a4/a44903e5fb107c1737c9ca1a6d0ba869784c7443b81068591bdb4ac0f7489555.svg) | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| ![](images/a6/a62d39bda1c2e3463ec07179055c47ac775369e9c0065725b9b607f70f56b68b.svg) | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| ![](images/73/73578072793a196046848a135a745d97d17a711987919e6cc8bb96cf9c9712e0.svg) | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| ![](images/7f/7fc91ddc948d8471c21fc38f7a4aca73204ca7ded9f5e867ae1dd2aa2dfde1f3.svg) | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084440 1064 "We have already seen how synthetic division can be used to quickly divide a polynomial by a linear binomial with a leading coefficient of $1$.\n\nIn fact, synthetic division can be used when the divisor is *any* linear binomial, like $(ax+b)$. We just factor out the leading coefficient $a$, carry out the division in the standard way, and then include the leading coefficient in the final result.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084440 1064 "We have already seen how synthetic division can be used to quickly divide a polynomial by a linear binomial with a leading coefficient of $1.$ \n\nIn fact, synthetic division can be used when the divisor is *any* linear binomial, like $(ax+b).$ We just factor out the leading coefficient $a,$ carry out the division in the standard way, and then include the leading coefficient in the final result.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084444 1064 "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4!$ (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24.\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t1! &= 1 \\[5pt]\n\t\t2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084444 1064 "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4$! (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1! &= 1 \\\\[5pt] 2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\[5pt] 3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\[5pt] 4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\\\[5pt] 5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1$:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084445 1064 "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results.\n\nFor example, computing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10!}{8!}\n$$\n\nusing this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way!\n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} {\\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that\n\n$$\n10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8\n$$\n\nand then cancel the $8$! that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084445 1064 "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results. \n\nFor example, computing $\\dfrac{10!}{8!}$ using this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90.\n\t}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way! \n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot  \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } { \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90\n\t}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that $10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8!$ and then cancel the $8!$ that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084466 1064 "Suppose that we want to divide two polynomials, such as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's get familiar with some terminology:\n\n- The **dividend** is the polynomial that we want to divide, so $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ in this case.\n- The **divisor** is the polynomial that we want to divide the dividend by, so $x-3$ in this case.\n- The result of the division, which will be a polynomial, is called the **quotient**.\n- The **remainder** is what is left after the division.\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In our case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to go!" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084466 1064 "Suppose that we want to divide two polynomials, such as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's get familiar with some terminology:\n\n- The ** dividend** is the polynomial that we want to divide, so $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ in this case.\n\n- The **divisor** is the polynomial that we want to divide the dividend by, so $x-3$ in this case.\n\n- The result of the division, which will be a polynomial, is called the **quotient**.\n\n- The **remainder** is what is left after the division.\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In our case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to go!" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084483 1064 "Dividing $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ by $x-3$, we obtained that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides by $x-3$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}=(x-3)(2x^2+x-4)-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, to check if we have done the division correctly, we can simply multiply the polynomials on the right-hand side and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)(2x^{2} + x - 4) - 1 &= x(2x^{2} + x - 4) - 3(2x^{2} + x - 4) - 1 \\\\\n&= 2x^{3} + x^{2} - 4x - 6x^{2} - 3x + 12 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2x^{3} - 5x^{2} - 7x + 11✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084483 1064 "Dividing $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ by $x-3,$ we obtained that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides by $x-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}=(x-3)(2x^2+x-4)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to check if we have done the division correctly, we can simply multiply the polynomials on the right-hand side and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 & =x(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-3(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-6{x}^{2}-3x+12-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-7x+11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084499 1064 "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f$. The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f$,** and denote by $f^{-1}$.\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f$. For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2)$, we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f$, that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that\n\n$$\nf({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2}\n$$\n\nand as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0$.\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f$:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186084499 1064 "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f.$ The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f,$** and denote by $f^{-1}.$\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f.$ For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2),$ we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}.\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f,$ that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that $f({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2},$  and as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0.$\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084500 1064 "Recall that the ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) \"reverses\" the action of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) so that\n![](images/ea/eab0458598a0b4298ccbfcd1664babf085309c0017ba0e530c76900f38bb8678.svg)\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3dd33f3f90eaadec2f1168db89665d1986e562005fc01cd9f58dbc3f17e7a616.png)\n\n \n\nSince ![](images/aa/aabc5ff96d4807ff058c8f3e9f78aa70ced5e624ac2ae1ebdd1bb4597d6c6dad.svg) is just the composition ![](images/5a/5aeac36928f2e7a97b7c71c2c97dd9bef3774970b3efc871c9131c6d2075be5e.svg) we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse. \n\nThe function ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) is called the **inverse of the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)** if\n![](images/52/52a4e32d6165988fe50c0766a1310fd3afb895f4a11149ff7b45c1cb0dd76e3a.svg)\nfor each ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) in the domain of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a9/a9e111e418e432fd154a819bb521c373ca64e9b27bbeed17a5777bba6940c6b6.svg) are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n![](images/7f/7facf5a55f1e90f9b9aac2358e4bc4b0560c2bd4b9fa2700e902a4dde84f90b7.svg)" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186084500 1064 "Recall that the $f^{-1}$ \"reverses\" the action of $f$ so that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9124176153ba77d905cf4cafad36d700f87ed5519a9f2e302b8a4dee6a6e1a51.png)\n\nSince $f^{-1}(f(x))$ is just the composition $(f^{-1} \\circ f)(x)$, we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse.\n\nThe function $f^{-1}$ is called the **inverse of the function $f$** if\n\n$$\n(f^{-1} \\circ f)(x) = x\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of $f$.\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n\n$$\n(g\\circ f)(x) = x\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521544 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521555 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186100953 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521537 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186114981 1069 "e-18993" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186114981 1069 "e-2629" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186114981 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles. If $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$ and $m \\angle COB = 50^\\circ$, find $m \\angle AOD$.\n\n![](images/60/60c48e8ce5fd289f6aa7b80cfd5a99db1adf9a0603e6162c1fb5b4dcf1a7e15d.png)" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186114981 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles. If $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$ and $m \\angle COB = 50^\\circ,$ find $m \\angle AOD.$\n\n![](images/60/60c48e8ce5fd289f6aa7b80cfd5a99db1adf9a0603e6162c1fb5b4dcf1a7e15d.png)" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186114981 1074 "Since $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOC + m∠COB &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠AOC + 50^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠AOC &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BOD = m \\angle AOC=40^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOD &= m∠AOC + m∠COB + m∠BOD \\\\\n&= 40^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 40^{∘} \\\\\n&= 130^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186114981 1074 "Since $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠COB & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOC+{50}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOC & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD,$ we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BOD = m \\angle AOC=40^\\circ .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOD & =m∠AOC+m∠COB+m∠BOD \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} \\\\  & ={130}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186115405 1039 "Solving Multi-Step Problems Involving Complementary Angles" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186115405 1039 "Using a Diagram and Properties of Complementary Angles to Find an Unknown Angle" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186115442 1043 #edb/ref 17592186115996 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186115442 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116386 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186115442 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116388 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186115442 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521612 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186115442 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521657 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186115442 1050 660.0 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186115996 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116387 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116385 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116386 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116385 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521657 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186116385 1061 8799 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116385 1061 484813 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186116386 1058 #edb/ref 17592186115996 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116387 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116388 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116387 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116389 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186116387 1061 8808 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116387 1061 484817 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186116388 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116389 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116389 1061 8813 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186116389 1