cobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186129634 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (2u + v) \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186129634 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v)$ and $y=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }v\\, & \\,\\text{ }u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2u+v}\\cdot u-\\frac{1}{2u+v}\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130937 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130938 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130940 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130941 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521000 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521012 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521035 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130936 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521199 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130936 1078 #edb/ref 17592186130939 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130962 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130963 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186130964 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521016 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521062 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130960 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521119 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186130960 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521062 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186131395 1078 #edb/ref 17592186131397 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186133926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521464 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186133927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521469 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186133928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521475 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186133951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521553 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134130 1071 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$,\nwhere $curlF = ⟨0, 0,-2y⟩$, and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ and the plane $z = 4 - x - 2y$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186134130 1071 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134130 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134134 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t) = \\sin (πt)i + \\cos (πt)j + \\sin (πt)k$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1)$.\nGiven that $F(x, y, z) = xi + yk$, evaluate the integral\n$∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186134134 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134134 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134226 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n \\cdot n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q$?\n\n1. $Q$ is invertible\n2. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n3. $P + Q$ is always orthogonal" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186134226 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. $Q$ is invertible\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $P+Q$ is always orthogonal" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186134226 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =PQ\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following sum of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example,it is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186147544 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521488 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186152124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521562 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159279 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159280 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159281 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159282 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159283 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520822 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520898 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521050 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521173 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521190 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186159278 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521190 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186162090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521518 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186162090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521527 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186162346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186162346 1071 "For the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186162346 1071 "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186162346 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186163306 1064 "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\nAA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n![](images/87/87581a69d3d76dbf3b0ebca8941c9a82b5b9443b00e86f32e4e9e4a42ef5e18b.svg)\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186163306 1064 "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\nAA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n![](images/87/87581a69d3d76dbf3b0ebca8941c9a82b5b9443b00e86f32e4e9e4a42ef5e18b.svg)\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186163314 1064 "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only.\n\nFor example, for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/d6/d6d6a167747f99c5a48be59ae3e11ca7b5a9188e80ddeb63b8c0f193d991b18c.svg)\n\nwe calculate its pseudoinverse as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Replace the non-zero elements on the main diagonal with their multiplicative inverses.\n\n- **Step 2:** Transpose the resulting matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186163314 1064 "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_