}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186168050 1064 "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A.$ \n![](images/c1/c12d2f59eb7eb9730fc7576bb426cff90a3cff4ac8577c3f212db8101197300b.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n![](images/4d/4dbe29e205fecf97bd7d4c0bfbf810c7336a6a50198fa35319e7ae8b0ac505c0.svg)\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186168051 1064 "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0$, the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i$. If\n\n$$\nx_i \\leq 0\n$$\n\nthe indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values\n\n$$\nx_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2\n$$\n\nUsing the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(3)1[x_{i} > 0] \\cdot x_{i} &= 1[5 > 0] \\cdot 5 + 1[- 3 > 0] \\cdot (-3) + 1[2 > 0] \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 5 + 0 \\cdot (-3) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 5 + 0 + 2 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]\n$$\n\ncounts the positive values. For this data, the count is\n\n$$\n1 + 0 + 1 = 2\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186168051 1064 "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0,$ the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i.$ If $x_i \\leq 0,$ the indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values $x_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2.$ Using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1[5>0]\\cdot 5+1[-3>0]\\cdot (-3)+1[2>0]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+0+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]$ counts the positive values. For this data, the count is $1 + 0 + 1 = 2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186168059 1064 "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A]\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A$.\n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n\n$$\n{1\\begin{vmatrix}ifx < 0 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}ifx \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186168059 1064 "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A].\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A.$ \n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n![](images/cd/cd01819adb510c2b439f44089b1bd3cbd1f49e671779039fdf2f6be62a39810f.svg)" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186168060 1064 "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n\n$$\n{x,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\geq 0[/math], \\\\ -x,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x < 0[/math]\n$$\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x)\n$$\n\nSince the conditions\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0$. This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid - 4 \\mid &= 1[- 4 \\ge 0] \\cdot (-4) + 1[- 4 < 0] \\cdot (- (-4)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186168060 1064 "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n![](images/33/336dc8080661db77e7ac701204aa6a32075bc7f4bd06625f4e8467e87a5c075c.svg)\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n|x| = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nSince the conditions $x \\geq 0$ and $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0.$ This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-4| & =1[-4\\ge 0]\\cdot (-4)+1[-4<0]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186171934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521495 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186172137 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521465 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186172138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521482 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186172138 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521541 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186184212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521467 13194139535595 