/ref 17592186521259 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521479 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521529 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521545 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188553 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n = 64$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $60$, and the sample standard deviation is $24$, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.656) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $63$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186188553 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=64$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $60,$ and the sample standard deviation is $24,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.656)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $63$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188553 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=60,\\,n=64,\\,s=24.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.656)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{63,0.005}=2.656.\n$$\n\n![](images/55/5596a286f89b2eed9fd6340c00e77915f84883f2d57d1b342b762143811537aa.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & \\pm 2.656\\cdot \\frac{24}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 60 & \\pm 7.968\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188558 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n = 100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $252$, and the sample standard deviation is $81$, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.984) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186188558 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $252,$ and the sample standard deviation is $81,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.984)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186188558 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=252,\\,n=100,\\,s=81.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.984)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{99,0.025}=1.984.\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b33840d7c98bbf799bdc58dc90d84752b3fa84c36d4a9b122a20ec9401b6823.png)\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}252 & \\pm 1.984\\cdot \\frac{81}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 252 & \\pm 16.070\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186202598 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521256 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186202598 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521256 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521455 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521460 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204010 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521471 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521520 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204442 1071 "Consider two sets of $25$ paired observations $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{25}$, and $y_{1}, y_{2}, …, y_{25}$. You are given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(25)x_{i} = 250, ∑_(i = 1)^(25)y_{i} = 265,; ∑_(i = 1)^(25)x_{i}^{2} = 3460, ∑_(i = 1)^(25)y_{i}^{2} = 5720$.\nCalculate the sum of the squared deviations from the mean for the points $x_{i}$.$S_{xx} =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186204442 1071 "Consider two sets of $25$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{25},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{25}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{x}_{i}=250,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{y}_{i}=265, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{x}_{i}^{2}=3\\,460,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{25}{y}_{i}^{2}=5\\,720.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the sum of the squared deviations from the mean for the points ${x}_{i}.$\n\n${S}_{xx}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204442 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & =3\\,460-\\frac{1}{25}(250)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,460-2\\,500 \\\\  & =960.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212725 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212726 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212727 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212728 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186212729 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520859 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520865 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521008 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521206 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521314 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186212724 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521206 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521484 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521532 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213823 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x + 2} + 4}$." 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186213823 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186213823 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+2}-4.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+2}-4,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-4}{\\sqrt{x+2}-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2}+4)(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x+2-16} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x-14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186281285 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2\\le z\\le 3y+x\\}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186281285 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,2\\le z\\le 3y+x}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186281299 1074 "![](images/bf/bf9c4790a8e31e000d2aeb7f7e557661683d3f52d737ba1b99abd407c15d6e99.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74954715928551ebb271cdfd461d783778b02b7944cbfd5502d6d06011408ad8.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3+x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3+x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3+x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3+x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3+x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4+2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[4x+{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8+4)-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186281299 1074 "![](images/bf/bf9c4790a8e31e000d2aeb7f7e557661683d3f52d737ba1b99abd407c15d6e99.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74954715928551ebb271cdfd461d783778b02b7944cbfd5502d6d06011408ad8.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3+x-y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3+x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3+x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3+x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3+x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3+x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4+2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[4x+{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8+4)-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186296734 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521082 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186296734 1078 #edb/ref 17592186296561 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186296734 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521082 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186412201 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-x\\le y\\le 1+x,\\,0\\le z\\le xy\\}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186412201 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-x\\le y\\le 1+x,\\,0\\le z\\le xy}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186500218 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520802 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186500218 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500052 13194139535595 17592186045417 false] [17592186500218 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520802 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521580 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521583 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521614 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521621 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1043 #edb/ref 17592186521642 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1045 "Stokes' Theorem" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1046 #edb/ref 17592186521621 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186520746 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [1759218652074