592186045417 true] [17592186521450 1077 "field-1" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521450 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520817 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521450 1079 #uuid "f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520930 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521065 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521109 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521144 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521149 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1077 "selection" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521149 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521451 1079 #uuid "fd517342-4196-5078-a01c-54f228cf9d0a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521379 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1069 "q-121321" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1071 "A man on a boat is sailing towards a $247\\,\\text{ft}$ high lighthouse. If the height of the man above the sea is $10\\,\\text{ft},$ how fast is the man approaching the lighthouse if his shadow is shrinking at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1072 #uuid "02ba8f64-b770-56fd-871d-5b790d9828ac" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521452 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/6c/6c85e0c34b34dd9dcb384bb601b9b87a1a6b7174888c9ad49d8c2cd37abc13de.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from the man to the lighthouse, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the lighthouse, and therefore the distance between him and the lighthouse *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{247} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =247y \\\\ 10x & =237y \\\\ x & =23.7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(23.7y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7(-0.25)≈-5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the man's speed is approximately $5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521390 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1069 "q-76576" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1071 "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1072 #uuid "07caf841-c23b-5d01-856b-51dc74025403" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521453 1074 "Using a calculator, we find \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =(41.810…)^{∘}+(26.565…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(68.375…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521361 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1069 "q-24805" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1071 "![](images/19/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1072 #uuid "08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521454 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521449 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1069 "q-251857" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1071 "Consider two sets of $40$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{40},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{40}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=54,\\,{S}_{yy}=35,\\,{S}_{xy}=188.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1072 #uuid "094cdd1f-e814-5164-bdf6-dc0f3e0f507e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521455 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=188.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{188}{40} \\\\  & =4.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521442 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1069 "q-2555" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1071 "![](images/89/8946fc7f3b9a087304d893d69196f8462913974aab552b05a442a7460a619bef.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1072 #uuid "0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521456 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{2}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521389 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1069 "q-116055" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1071 "![](images/eb/eb40860015e5404f3680b22835149ddc63baf80cd8e1f34e30f936308c3221e0.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1072 #uuid "0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521457 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521435 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1069 "q-344484" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1071 "The members of a robotics club are testing a small robot in a courtyard lit by only one large lamp post.  Sam, a $1.4\\,\\text{m}$ student, is walking away from the post, and his shadow is increasing at a rate of $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ If the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than  Sam, how fast is  Sam walking away from the post?\n\nSam's velocity is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1072 #uuid "0f5cbdde-4826-5bb5-88c4-7a602876e76a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521458 1074 "Since we are given that the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than Sam, the height of the post is $7+1.4=8.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nLet's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/1c/1c17902179842d470f95519599235a9998e4cfbe538a44c201e34c3627c493af.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is Sam's distance from the lamp post, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the length of Sam's shadow changes at the rate\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the rate of change of the length of Sam's shadow is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp post, and therefore, the length of the shadow *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8.4} & =\\frac{y}{1.4} \\\\ 1.4x+1.4y & =8.4y \\\\ 1.4x & =7y \\\\ x & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5y) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\cdot (0.8) \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sam's velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521415 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1069 "q-49014" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1071 "![](images/35/3556e6b8bda17ab8242b8cd7924e1f5ad975efcbc6c5235667d786b2f0908e1d.png)\n\nTrain $A$ travels east from station $S$ at a constant velocity of $153\\,\\text{km/h},$ and train $B$ travels south toward $S.$ The distance between the trains is increasing at a rate of $35\\text{ km/h}.$  What is the speed of train $B$ when train $A$ is $51\\,\\text{km}$ from the station and train $B$ is $68\\,\\text{km}$ from the station?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1072 #uuid "0fa1eef6-69ad-599c-9e20-68839bce41b1" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521459 1074 "![](images/81/81d16bb54bdecca628d769cd08d02dd327c5483851c2421fb595446c34f8a124.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the station and train $A,$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the station and train $B,$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two trains.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=51\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=68\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {51}^{2}+{68}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =7\\,225 \\\\ z & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=153\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=35\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(51)(153)+2(68)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(85)(35) \\\\ 136\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,950-15\\,606 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9\\,656}{136} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of train $B$ is $71\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521341 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1069 "q-251861" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1071 "Consider two sets of $15$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{15}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=105,\\,{S}_{yy}=81,\\,{S}_{xy}=69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1072 #uuid "1470b28d-5083-563f-9b26-d7812411f35b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521460 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=69.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{69}{15} \\\\  & =4.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521378 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1069 "q-346657" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[7\\in \\{2,4,6,10\\}].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1072 #uuid "1501d1dd-a30a-5622-a170-02307bfe9a8b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521461 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $7\\in \\{2,4,6,10\\}.$ Since $7$ is not an element of the set $\\{2,4,6,10\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[7\\in \\{2,4,6,10\\}]=0.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521364 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1069 "q-2588" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1071 "![](images/77/7741295c73895ec39933adec100c9f3fc7e803fa8ea2a02dae543477c45cbea4.png)\n\nA boat $B$ is being pulled in toward a dock $D$ with a rope by a fisherman $F$ standing on the dock. His hands are $4\\text{ ft}$ above the ground, and he is pulling the rope at a rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}$, how fast is the distance between the dock and the boat decreasing when the boat is $3\\text{ft}$ from the dock?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1072 #uuid "15178b94-38d6-597d-bc8c-39fd354b56c7" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521462 1074 "![](images/af/afbe02b63ca4d45ae4d1759d7fa452b57a8b43f073d08b1368f51472e2e4ea8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the dock and the boat,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the boat mooring.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{ft }.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(0) & =2(5)(-3) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-30 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the dock and the boat is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521406 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1069 "q-59899" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1071 "![](images/6e/6e12ca457954f93778b772f2d0bd5a1e63e7e8e7eaf626d309300e97b23ab525.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1072 #uuid "162ad66e-4fa5-5917-937f-4dfd0e95e3fd" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521463 1074 "First, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $4$ hexagons.\n\nThen, we count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $2$ rhombuses.\n\nSo the ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $4:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:1$ is equivalent to $4:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $2:1$ because there are $2$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521387 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1069 "q-162232" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1072 #uuid "19360ab0-63c4-5cd8-9a4a-b29910944cd3" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521464 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-1\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{({t}^{2}-t)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521424 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1069 "q-53700" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1071 "Write the equation of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}-8x-14$ in vertex form." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1072 #uuid "1b44bc96-863e-5a73-a2ed-628d5188e295" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521465 1074 "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}-8x-14=-2({x}^{2}+4x+7)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+4x+7) & = \\\\ -2((x+2)^{2}+3) & = \\\\ -2(x+2)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is $y=-2(x+2)^{2}-6.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521420 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1069 "q-161694" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1071 "![](images/43/4315e0e20d37935d3f5454063475fdfa5ed7a1915f82ae96194e83880ba02e76.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1072 #uuid "1f0b5299-7a35-5c1a-9b72-d6af14bd952f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521466 1074 "![](images/0b/0ba756dacf72b89b06c3c214e1582a74fa803f567082f57538e7a78c84d4591f.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+x-y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+x-y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=2+x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(2+x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}+x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3+2)-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521417 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1069 "q-140476" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1071 "Rewrite $-8{z}^{2}-12z-\\frac{7}{2}$ by completing the square." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1072 #uuid "20662b40-9142-5228-b622-edf8a16af72c" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521467 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{16}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}) & =-8({(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521343 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1069 "q-30330" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1071 "Harry shines a flashlight on a $10\\,\\text{in}$ tall cat approaching him. If he is holding the flashlight $30\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and the animal's shadow is changing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is the cat approaching Harry?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1072 #uuid "21055998-26d2-5a03-b3c0-e38582a8d839" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521468 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/02/021a84ea0ad36014569028c0ffb39ab9b30f9225cf4bfcccba2d60050754a6f9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between Harry and the cat, while $y$ is the length of the cat's shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the velocity of the cat's shadow\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the velocity of the cat's shadow is *negative* because the cat is walking *toward* Harry, and therefore the distance between Harry and the cat *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{30} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =30y \\\\ 10x & =20y \\\\ x & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2(-11)=-22\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cat is $22\\,\\text{in/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521421 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1069 "q-162363" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ $y=\\pm 2.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1072 #uuid "22c4166d-7812-5cc0-89d7-bc30157982f0" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521469 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521430 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1069 "q-92941" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nIn order to make some flannels, Martha bought $5$ meters of cotton fabric and $4$ meters of linen fabric, so there are $\\boxed{}$ meters of cotton fabric for each meter of linen fabric." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1072 #uuid "2668924b-c4c3-5180-8799-da87f8fa9aec" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521470 1074 "The ratio of the amount of cotton fabric to the amount of linen fabric is $5:4.$ \n\nWe can rewrite this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThis means that there are $\\frac{5}{4}$ meters of cotton fabric for each meter of linen fabric." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521400 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1069 "q-251898" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1071 "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $9$ | $12$ | $10$ | $15$ | $12$ | $21$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{y}_{i}^{2}=144,$ calculate ${S}_{yy}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1072 #uuid "26b0163b-edde-5aab-b2bd-a06d38a87337" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521471 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${y}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{y}_{i}=3+4+5+4+7+2+3+4=32.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{y}_{i}^{2}=144.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{yy}=144-\\frac{1}{8}(32)^{2}=144-128=16.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521410 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1069 "q-118674" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1071 "A bus is traveling at night at $50\\,\\text{km/h}.$  After running past a $6.25\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost, the bus driver notices that the shadow of the bus is growing. \nIf the height of the bus is $3.75\\,m,$ how fast is the length of the shadow growing while it travels away from the lamp?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1072 #uuid "28170d6f-d1bf-54a6-9dab-69ada5f8b07e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521472 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data. \n\n![](images/10/1094154aa39b3d9f1206d23ca920d5717a7f344c4a9486e9c4604752cf0ce623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the bus' distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the runner's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the bus's velocity is *positive* because it is moving *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the bus and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6.25} & =\\frac{y}{3.75} \\\\ 3.75x+3.75y & =6.25y \\\\ 3.75x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =1.5\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =75\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the bus' shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{km/h}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521426 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1069 "q-123549" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{9})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{50}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{80}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{9})$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1072 #uuid "299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521473 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${50}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{50}^{∘}=50\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${80}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{80}^{∘}=80\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{2\\pi }{9}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521411 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1069 "q-104079" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1071 "![](images/9d/9dbee02f0c07041dd8424faf683952ada2f7f1a604f9bf89a65588eaf3b6f9f6.png)\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are increasing. The length, measured in feet, of each leg is given by $x(t)=t+{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the length of the hypotenuse after $2$ seconds? Give your answer rounded to $1$ decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1072 #uuid "2bda6d09-1af6-5a1c-86e9-4f34148c24fa" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521474 1074 "In the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the equal sides, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $x,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the length of the equal sides after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=(2)+(2)^{2}=6\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 72 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & ≈8.485\\,3\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rate of change of the sides is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=2t+1.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocity is\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=2(2)+1=5\\text{ ft/s}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(5)+2(6)(5) & ≈2(8.485\\,3)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & ≈7.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hypotenuse's length is changing at a rate of $7.1\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521451 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1069 "q-162890" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1072 #uuid "2bdcc65b-8401-5044-8aa5-c9a7e37ffa6b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521475 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521409 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1069 "q-346654" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[0\\le 3].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1072 #uuid "2c6fa2f7-da40-5cad-be4c-16622f82a062" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521476 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $0\\le 3.$ Since $0$ is less than or equal to $3,$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[0\\le 3]=1.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521408 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1069 "q-344428" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1071 "A $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall student is jogging away from a $4.0\\,\\text{m}$ parking lot light at $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the length of the student’s shadow changing?\n\nThe length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1072 #uuid "2f7c2967-af65-5004-9025-62af739a76dd" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521477 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e5/e500e84e039b0f8bef6afc7e636d6142309d65f13017c1f817fdf159b3f4d77c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the student’s distance from the parking lot light, while $y$ is the length of their shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the student’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the student’s velocity is *positive* because they are jogging *away* from the parking lot light, and therefore, the distance between the student and the light *increases* with time. \n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4.0} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =4.0y \\\\ 1.5x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1.5}{2.5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{25}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & ≈1.4\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately $1.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521425 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1069 "q-346659" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1072 #uuid "30559f45-859e-514a-9084-78b866aea583" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521478 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}.$ Since $9$ is not an element of the set $\\{1,2,3,5\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}]=0.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521353 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1069 "q-142975" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1071 "In a particular state, $400$ randomly selected working adults were asked the distance from their home to their workplace. The sample mean distance was $8.52\\,\\text{mi}$ with standard deviation $2.40\\,\\text{mi}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the mean distance from home to work  $\\mu $ for all working adults in this state.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1072 #uuid "33652587-e008-57dc-9a3a-905f5c4ae6f4" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521479 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8.52\\,\\text{mi},\\,n=400,\\,s=2.40\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{399,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{399,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=400-1=399$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/82/829fa68b18545ff59ffa702fe4567997d635089edf4e90d713b1c8d817c7959f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{399,0.005}=2.588:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $2.588$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.52 & \\pm 2.588\\cdot \\frac{2.40}{\\sqrt{400}}\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ 8.52 & \\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521355 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1069 "q-314510" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1071 "![](images/00/007ac521cd9bd97773aa18e243e788ce9eb473a65e3242022539266bc8f23ef4.png)\n\nA laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are $12.5\\,\\text{mm}$ long each?\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1072 #uuid "37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521480 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.5.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4\\cdot 12.5=-30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square aperture is decreasing at a rate of $30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521347 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1069 "q-104077" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1071 "After attending school, Elizabeth and Jessica leave at the same time. Elizabeth heads north at a speed of $4.5\\text{ mph},$ while Jessica heads east at a speed of $6\\text{ mph}.$ How fast are the two moving away from each other after walking for $2$ hours?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1072 #uuid "385d74bf-db85-520e-88d9-f555fc3d02fe" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521481 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/2b/2b012a853cf973cc68df35bbd94a498fd1d0d907548c1f6d8ca3f842c967c877.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Jessica and the school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Elizabeth and the school, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine Elizabeth's distance from school and Jessica's distance from school after $2$ hours. Since their velocities are constants, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=4.5(2)=9\\text{ mi}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=6(2)=12\\text{ mi}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)(4.5)+2(12)(6) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 225 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica increases at a rate of $7.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after walking $2$ hours." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521404 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1069 "q-13411" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1071 "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is $(-5,4)$ and leading coefficient is $2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1072 #uuid "39734cdd-98b1-5bc5-a3ba-0e209592784a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521482 1074 "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,4)$ and $a=2$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(x-(-5))^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2(x+5)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+10x+25)+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+50+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y=2{x}^{2}+20x+54.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521448 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1069 "q-68030" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.9)$ in radians?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1072 #uuid "39cecc3d-6cf0-5c7c-a0ca-fadfcdb5000c" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521483 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.9)=1.119\\,769…≈1.120\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521433 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1069 "q-296017" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1072 #uuid "39e0f80f-2c55-5d22-8bb4-e3a1b6fcdd0a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521484 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{3x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3x}+\\sqrt{5})}{3x-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521372 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1069 "q-296007" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1072 #uuid "3a9fdb79-91cf-5159-aced-4b759a444970" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521485 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z})(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{10z})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{2-10z} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{2(1-5z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521385 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1069 "q-241885" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1071 "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.64$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha =1.25$, where $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ are acute angles measured in degrees, then $\\theta +\\alpha $ in degrees to one decimal place is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1072 #uuid "3d28482b-a0c8-53e6-a980-b5acf56e83ea" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521486 1074 "To find $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ we use $arccos$ and $arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0.64 \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\  & =(50.208\\,1…)^{∘}+(51.340\\,1…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(101.548\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{101.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521382 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1069 "q-171123" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1071 "$(5.2)\\cdot (-3)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1072 #uuid "3e520184-edf6-56d7-ae3f-333c3748146f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521487 1074 "We have a product of a positive number $(5.2)$ and a negative number $(-3).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n5.2\\cdot (-3)=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $5.2$ by $3$ without any signs: $5.2\\cdot 3=15.6.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n5.2\\cdot (-3)=-15.6.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521365 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1069 "q-350258" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1071 "Ethan and Lucas are swimmers. In the previous season, $75%$ of Ethan’s races were freestyle, while the decimal proportion of Lucas’s races that were freestyle was $0.4.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\nII. More than half of Lucas's races were freestyle.\n\nIII. Ethan had a greater percentage of freestyle races compared to Lucas." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1072 #uuid "3e597010-3568-5f92-b7bf-8866f8549553" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521488 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n75%>50%,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Lucas's races were freestyle.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $75%$ and $0.4$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince $75%>40%,$ Ethan had a **greater** percentage of freestyle races compared to Lucas.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521395 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1069 "q-19873" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{89!\\,82!}{90!\\,81!}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1072 #uuid "3e9e4e75-7918-575f-a18d-8c831116251f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521489 1074 "First, notice that $90!=90\\cdot 89!$ and $82!=82\\cdot 81!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $89!$ and $81!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{89!\\,82!}{90!\\,81!} & =\\frac{89!\\,\\cdot 82\\cdot 81!}{90\\cdot 89!\\,\\cdot 81!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{89!\\,}\\cdot 82\\cdot \\cancel{81!}}{90\\cdot \\cancel{89!\\,}\\cdot \\cancel{81!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{90} \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521358 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1069 "q-2942" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1071 "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $12\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $16\\text{ m}?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1072 #uuid "44d55ece-0880-515f-a521-353e6c740d6b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521490 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/ee/eee3de464da57671142e2677974b44c63dc2397215ffdd2aa36e2c3d401a647d.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {12}^{2}+{16}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$  when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(16)(-2) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 8 & =40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521351 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1069 "q-346661" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1071 "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{B}(4)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1072 #uuid "45172067-6fd8-53b2-8b3e-3767eb3b99d5" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521491 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{B}(x)$ of a set $B$ equals $1$ if $x\\in B$ and $0$ if $x\\notin B.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(x)=1[x\\in B].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(4)=1[4\\in B]=1[4\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}].\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521413 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1069 "q-346664" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1071 "Let $E=\\{1,4,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{E}(9)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1072 #uuid "4946e8ca-eef4-5bca-ab77-2e318993cb3a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521492 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{E}(x)$ of a set $E$ equals $1$ if $x\\in E$ and $0$ if $x\\notin E.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(x)=1[x\\in E].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(9)=1[9\\in E]=1[9\\in \\{1,4,9\\}].\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521412 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1069 "q-60088" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1071 "![](images/2d/2d4fc837b149453ef874950260279781e57ca398b92023e28ebe4fb3b841c311.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing numbers in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to kites is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $\\,\\boxed{}\\,$ rectangles for every $5$ kites." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1072 #uuid "4be9a2f5-2f4a-5a28-99ac-bf9fd6c5ffaf" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521493 1074 "We can express the ratio of the number of rectangles to the number of kites as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{number of rectangles}:\\text{number of kites}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of rectangles. There are $2$ rectangles.\n\n- Next, we count the number of kites. There are $5$ kites.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of rectangles to kites is $2:5.$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $5$ and $2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521381 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1069 "q-123545" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{57}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{60})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{35})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{14}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{80})$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1072 #uuid "4cd34dd2-403c-55c2-9000-3c54447738a1" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521494 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${57}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{57}^{∘}=57\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{60}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${32}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{32}^{∘}=32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{8\\pi }{35}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${14}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{14}^{∘}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{7\\pi }{80}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521436 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1069 "q-223451" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $631$\n\nII. $782$\n\nIII. $686$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1072 #uuid "4d3c9434-4ef2-5a17-95da-683191ed9ce8" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521495 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $631,$ we have that \n\n$$\n63-2(1)=63-2=61\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $631$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $782,$ we have that \n\n$$\n78-2(2)=78-4=74\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $782$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $686,$ we have that \n\n$$\n68-2(6)=68-12=56\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $686$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521356 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1069 "q-73019" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{8!\\,5!}{3!\\,4!\\,6!}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1072 #uuid "4e0bd81c-fffc-5b90-b2d5-10d251044393" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521496 1074 "First, notice that $8!=8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6!$ and $5!=5\\cdot 4!$ . Then, we can cancel the $6!$ and $4!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8!\\,5!}{3!\\,4!\\,6!} & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6!\\cdot 5\\cdot 4!}{3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{3!\\cdot \\cancel{4!}\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 5}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{140}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521437 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1069 "q-116049" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1071 "![](images/bb/bbd79b56420475595834e8cd67d4b9d3d5ca8a2e0fda399f49de1f4e9bb26c95.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square increase at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour}$, at what rate is the perimeter changing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1072 #uuid "5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521497 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(4\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=16\\text{ feet/hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square increases at $16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521338 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1069 "q-123537" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{15}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{36})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{35}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{36})$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1072 #uuid "5168a992-4557-5020-911a-67c9df9985ed" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521498 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${15}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{15}^{∘}=15\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${35}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{35}^{∘}=35\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{36}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521383 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1069 "q-313985" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1071 "A spotlight $S$ is on the ground $6\\,\\text{m}$ away from a wall and a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall robot is moving towards the wall at a rate of $0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the robot is $2.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the spotlight?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1072 #uuid "53b5eaef-0c6b-5313-b037-03a8dbcbc92d" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521499 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e9/e91c4e08aa785de1fe500c2f43e0981501ee597bb7bb042827154842ec06ad0c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the robot’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the robot’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the robot’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(6-x)+x} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{9}{6-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{(6-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=6-2.5=3.5\\,\\text{m}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{{2.5}^{2}}\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521419 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1069 "q-35203" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1071 "![](images/d8/d8788f03a4cb07d8f447346699ed5a9e8231cc4e496e135b6df1515e56792d61.png)\n\nThe graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $f(x)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1072 #uuid "56462713-a3e2-5f05-8120-8de6685511d4" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521500 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddf15053fee30ed69e2caed1a049b34c6f39c729fec0f1a053b00e33686c1e5f.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,6) & ⟶(6,0) \\\\ (1,4) & ⟶(4,1) \\\\ (2,-2) & ⟶(-2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521377 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1069 "q-99753" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1071 "What is $0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1072 #uuid "57848bee-3e72-5d15-a97c-92bd970ffedc" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521501 1074 "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})=0.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521427 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1069 "q-122025" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1071 "$9!=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1072 #uuid "5bf1583d-22a0-55d5-823f-9b4dd8cd99b3" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521502 1074 "Computing $9!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=362\\,880.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521450 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1069 "q-344456" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1072 #uuid "5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521503 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=24\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521369 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1069 "q-86134" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.71)$ in radians?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1072 #uuid "5ebed1ea-4030-56bb-a75b-85ec95570cad" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521504 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.71)=0.789\\,498…≈0.789\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521384 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1069 "q-54420" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1071 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(1)=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1072 #uuid "5f0cef98-4d6e-5f8f-803e-692ed6a93d90" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521505 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}≈1.850\\,815…≈1.851\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521394 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1069 "q-66451" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1071 "![](images/72/724d629662e879a2d57f920eb4fe8c3cfc44f9f755d2ae5dca10d5605bab0f9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1072 #uuid "5f4136a2-4487-503b-8a03-26f4549cc680" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521506 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/17/17b769a94abc2035d5d24e82c65d9c8fd6252152482826c3a34e8708d246c213.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ -2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521357 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1069 "q-5735" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1071 "| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-10$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nGiven the function table above, what does ${g}^{-1}(-3)$ equal?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1072 #uuid "606d0246-f6d2-5866-ae11-21bc32e2c786" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521507 1074 "From the table, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-1)=-3\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-1)) & ={g}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ -1 & ={g}^{-1}(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(-3)=-1.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521333 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1069 "q-344457" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $20{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1072 #uuid "6378a8a6-ced0-5ab5-b937-c9000ce1db16" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521508 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=20\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521328 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1069 "q-4546" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1071 "![](images/40/404e9d30d45485251a19cdb2ccfef4ea53509a66437c99908eda065ad99e5e82.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1072 #uuid "63ad50c7-f590-525f-9856-7ab603587d0d" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521509 1074 "Since we are given the lengths of the opposite side and the hypotenuse, we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =(27.486\\,4…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{27.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521440 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1069 "q-344354" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1071 "In a physics experiment, the net charge on a sensor (in $mC$) after $t$ seconds is given by the function $Q(t).$ The values of $Q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.0$ | $1.5$ | $2.0$ | $2.5$ | $3.0$ | $3.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$Q(t)$** | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n\nApproximate the sensor’s charge instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.5.$\n\nThe sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately \n{{field-1}} $mC$ per second." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1072 #uuid "6feb34ac-08d5-5bc5-ac07-3b8f7d9f2f78" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521510 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{Q(t+h)-Q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.5$ with step size $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(2.5) & ≈\\frac{Q(2.5+0.5)-Q(2.5-0.5)}{2(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q(3.0)-Q(2.0)}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2}{1.0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{1.0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sensor’s charge at $t=2.5$ is decreasing at approximately $3\\,mC$ per second." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521352 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1069 "q-18648" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1071 "$\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1072 #uuid "74edae7f-921c-52ab-b265-0f5c05fcd5a7" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521511 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1} & =\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 8 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & -4 & 0 & 1 \\\\  & 8 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{2}(8{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}=4{x}^{2}-1.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521445 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1069 "q-346656" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1072 #uuid "77a3c45c-df66-5438-9ce6-8791cf4f16da" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521512 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}.$ Since $3$ is an element of the set $\\{1,3,6,8\\},$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}]=1.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521441 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1069 "q-344787" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1071 "A spherical hailstone is growing as it moves through a storm cloud. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the hailstone is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the hailstone at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1072 #uuid "7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521513 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & =(12\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521397 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1069 "q-5754" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1071 "![](images/d4/d43d45e070fe27d03717c2c88740b4536a23c7adba0ba2aa2ae451ecab34dbb5.png)\n\nWhat are the $xy$-coordinates of the point shown in the axes above?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1072 #uuid "785b3e3d-2e78-5863-9118-440c52c1081d" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521514 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point is $5$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, the point has an $x$-coordinate of $-5.$\n\nAdditionally, the point is $7$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, the point has a $y$-coordinate of $-7.$\n\nThe point has an $x$-coordinate of $-5$ and a $y$-coordinate of $-7,$ so its coordinates are $(-5,-7).$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521388 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1069 "q-347932" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=6,\\,{x}_{4}=-2,\\,{x}_{5}=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1072 #uuid "7ce60c02-eb25-536f-a376-c9d5685f21ea" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521515 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2$ equals ${x}_{i}-1$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $2$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2\n$$\n\nadds ${x}_{i}-1$ for nonnegative values and $2$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-3\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[1\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[6\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[-2\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2 & =2+(1-1)+(6-1)+2+(0-1) \\\\  & =2+0+5+2-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521371 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1069 "q-122419" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1071 "Find the value of  $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1072 #uuid "7d8ea2fc-3731-5b48-8288-39aacc74f9f4" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521516 1074 "Using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & =(56.309…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521359 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1069 "q-75127" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1071 "![](images/b7/b76466e577343a234b53566b00939a3de312ad4f71106ebf3cbed3bc9d8795ea.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=8$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1072 #uuid "7efd0b4a-5765-528c-b6d9-1a45ad254c36" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521517 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=8$ with step size $h=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(8) & ≈\\frac{f(8+4)-f(8-4)}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(12)-f(4)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521362 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1069 "q-138484" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1071 "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1072 #uuid "7eff1b38-24fd-5e4c-adf3-ec6e1162e257" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521518 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521368 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1069 "q-54375" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1071 "Given that $x=0.5$ radians, what is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1072 #uuid "840c078c-4570-5c8b-92a3-92eaaeeaa79e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521519 1074 "Note the argument of the cosine is given in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.5)=0.877\\,582…≈0.878\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521335 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1069 "q-251802" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1071 "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $12$ | $4$ | $6$ | $10$ | $8$ | $15$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $20$ | $10$ | $5$ | $1$ | $7$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=535,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1072 #uuid "84b31519-3642-53d1-a74c-ac68de0d653b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521520 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =12+4+6+10+8+15+11=66, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =20+10+5+1+7+4+9=56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=535.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=535-\\frac{1}{7}(66)(56)=535-528=7.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521391 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1069 "q-78657" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4,\\,g(\\sqrt{3})=-7,\\,g(4)=-6,\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${g}^{-1}(4).$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1072 #uuid "8bc675ed-3657-5d25-80e1-2c0715c7f4ff" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521521 1074 "From the given information, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-\\sqrt{2})) & ={g}^{-1}(4) \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & ={g}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(4)=-\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521386 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521398 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1069 "q-218663" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1071 "Rewrite $-({z}^{2}+z+2)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({z}^{2}+z+2)=-(z\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1072 #uuid "8c5fa084-327e-5d66-96c0-960c479aa7b9" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521522 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+z+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({z}^{2}+z+2) & =-({(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521367 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1069 "q-115973" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1071 "A child on a scooter is running away from a $345\\,\\text{cm}$ tall lamppost at $4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ The height of the child on the scooter is $96.6\\,cm.$\nHow fast is the length of the child's shadow changing?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1072 #uuid "8cb404ed-6af7-5b0d-8507-6193eb5d4f21" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521523 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/fe/fefe072c51da577b504d81ccd1bb3546e77bca9bf9885950ca0c0c2091f9d335.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the child's distance from the lamppost, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the child's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the child's velocity is *positive* because he is running *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the child and the lamppost *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{345} & =\\frac{y}{96.6} \\\\ 96.6x+96.6y & =345y \\\\ 96.6x & =248.4y \\\\ y & =\\frac{966}{2484}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{18}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{7}{18}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot (4.5) \\\\  & =1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the child's shadow increases at approximately $1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521418 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1069 "q-115924" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1071 "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to ten times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the length of the sides of the square at that moment?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1072 #uuid "8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521524 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521443 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1069 "q-66616" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(5,1)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1072 #uuid "8dc02ab1-2232-53a5-8f5c-8a9183793c53" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521525 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(5,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(1,5)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521439 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1069 "q-344508" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1071 "Ethan is walking toward a $360\\,\\text{in}$ light pole, which has a lamp on top. If Ethan's height is $72\\,\\text{in}$ and his shadow is changing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is he walking toward the light pole?\n\nEthan is moving at a speed of \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1072 #uuid "91d63402-0442-54f2-9dc3-353df5f34caa" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521526 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4e82a1365099bcf76ae91b75d0d1ad276e4c35f8b172fc60b4d22e9156128d7e.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from Ethan to the light pole, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that Ethan's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that Ethan's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the light pole, and therefore the distance between him and the light pole *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{360} & =\\frac{y}{72} \\\\ 72x+72y & =360y \\\\ 72x & =288y \\\\ x & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4(-6)=-24\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ethan's speed is  $24\\,\\text{in/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521432 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1069 "q-138486" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1071 "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1072 #uuid "92a0ecc9-adbd-5716-8a7f-f658ffcbaf41" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521527 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521376 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1069 "q-54418" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1071 "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1072 #uuid "934b60d8-f889-5c91-8ad2-ede254ef4d64" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521528 1074 "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521330 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1069 "q-143028" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1071 "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $50\\,\\text{mph},$ on a standard test car. A sample of $25$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $174\\,\\text{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Assuming that the stopping distances are normally distributed, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu $ of these tires.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $\\,1.711\\,$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1072 #uuid "969c1777-ec8b-5e40-b43c-413b46fc477f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521529 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/52/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521329 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1069 "q-344674" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1071 "![](images/34/34a41e39a36d3e073ef2ca4144bd346b340efa22a9da7d3e8a9df48d32d900b3.png)\n\nA square opening in a testing chamber is being reduced, causing the area of the opening to decrease at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals decreasing when each diagonal measures $35\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1072 #uuid "9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521530 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square opening, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-49.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=35.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot (-49) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{49}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.4\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the opening is decreasing at a rate of \n$1.4\\text{ mm/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521434 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1069 "q-344494" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1071 "A bright lantern $L$ is on the ground $18\\,\\text{ft}$ away from a wall and a $6\\,\\text{ft}$ tall actor is moving towards the wall at a rate of $3\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the actor is $8\\,\\text{ft}$ from the lantern?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1072 #uuid "98780b5d-6c50-5c2a-867a-a1e2a96de8f6" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521531 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0a/0a47e801db651c027415a7943e5327bd378c1a08e533dc69673ab4304c8cc7b4.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the actor’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the actor’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the actor’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(18-x)+x} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{18} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{108}{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{108}{18-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{(18-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=18-8=10\\,\\text{ft}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521416 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1069 "q-111099" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1072 #uuid "992f7e69-958f-50e1-a735-f09f05b61b6f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521532 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}}{\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4})(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(\\sqrt{r+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{r+4})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(r+2)-(r+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(-2)} & = \\\\ -5(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521396 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1069 "q-73320" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1072 #uuid "9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521533 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521345 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1069 "q-47536" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1071 "![](images/4b/4b280f9756119d26317899e07e9a23b3679555701b23096516afb7a646cda453.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=5$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1072 #uuid "9a46b6ba-aacd-5c29-a1ea-c98b8e558d24" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521534 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=5$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{f(5+1)-f(5-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521407 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1069 "q-123503" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.8)$ in radians?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1072 #uuid "9b89dfe3-da21-530c-903d-947ec6af3854" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521535 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.8)=0.643\\,501…≈0.644\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521373 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1069 "q-124397" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1071 "$\\frac{4!}{0!}=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1072 #uuid "9ddd958f-3ed8-5647-8898-4f321748c5a0" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521536 1074 "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $4!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n4!=4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=24.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4!}{0!} & =\\frac{24}{1}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521402 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1069 "q-100100" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1071 "![](images/3c/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1072 #uuid "a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521537 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=6$  and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=2.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))=6+2(2)=10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))=1+2(2)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521336 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1069 "q-4743" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1071 "![](images/61/61dc032461e469146f11eacac4f917e86d40b3078dc2d33ac89e8e0b31eefd48.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1072 #uuid "a9231519-804c-5398-8af6-73a4e2d373bf" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521538 1074 "Since we are given the lengths of the adjacent side and the hypotenuse, we can use cosine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{10}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{27} \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =(68.261\\,5…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{68.3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521393 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1069 "q-161660" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1072 #uuid "b01530fa-080b-571d-ad1e-cd03aad8ce73" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521539 1074 "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type III region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-{x}^{2}}[y]_{y=-1}^{y=x}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-{x}^{2}}(x-(-1))\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-{x}^{2}}x+1\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+1){[z]}_{z=0}^{z=2-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+1)(2-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x+2\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}+1+2)-(-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}+1-2) \\\\  & =2(2-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521428 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1069 "q-346652" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9<3].$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1072 #uuid "b1a403c5-0788-5593-a233-1f2c8a521c2c" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521540 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9<3.$ Since $9$ is not less than $3,$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9<3]=0.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521392 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1069 "q-106267" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1071 "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is at $(-8,-20)$ and whose leading coefficient is $1.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1072 #uuid "b1cfb6c9-bb07-5ff5-8e41-4388a89b7e12" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521541 1074 "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-20)$ and $a=1$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1(x-(-8))^{2}-20 \\\\  & =(x+8)^{2}-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+64-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y={x}^{2}+16x+44.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521431 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1069 "q-314491" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1071 "![](images/e8/e8944ed32d3019a0d0d1ccebbc2f40c5248c221f3cddba237906db509fac0737.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $8\\text{ ft/s}.$ At what rate is the area of ​​the square increasing when the side is $4\\,\\text{ft}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1072 #uuid "b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521542 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{ft/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{ft},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\cdot 2(4) \\\\  & =64{\\text{ ft}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521337 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1069 "q-158088" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ and $g(x)=kx.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1072 #uuid "b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521543 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =kx \\\\ g(f(x)) & =kf(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =kf(x) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{x}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{8}x & =x \\\\ \\frac{k}{8}\\cancel{x} & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ \\frac{k}{8} & =1 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521374 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1069 "q-300738" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1071 "![](images/b5/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1072 #uuid "bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521544 1074 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x)=2.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-4)^{2}+1)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =66\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521429 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1069 "q-142976" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1071 "The amount of hemoglobin in the bloodstream of people living in a particular Colorado community follows a normal distribution. An analysis on a random sample of $16$ people yields a mean hemoglobin concentration of $13.1\\,\\text{g/dl}$ with a sample standard deviation of $1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean hemoglobin concentration $\\mu $ for all people in the community.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1072 #uuid "bb725276-ca73-5e90-be79-e741b1537c3e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521545 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521331 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1069 "q-73369" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1071 "![](images/8b/8b4196d82bf27236e6c28651a01678c60c41bc851afbb7aad2e754578ba330e9.png)\n\nJacob is a carpenter. He wishes to reduce the size of a wooden square while maintaining its shape. The lengths of the diagonals of the square decrease at a rate of $2\\text{ cm}\\text{/h}.$ At what rate, in squared centimeters per hour, is the area of ​​the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $45\\,\\text{cm}?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1072 #uuid "c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521546 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/h},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=45.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 45=-90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521340 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1069 "q-27912" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1071 "If $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =0.25$ and $\\theta $ is acute, then the value of $\\theta $ in degrees to one decimal place is" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1072 #uuid "c9e6b263-d73b-5513-9574-32caadf21e21" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521547 1074 "To find $\\theta $ we use $arctan.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =0.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25). \\\\  & =(14.036\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{14.0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521447 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1069 "q-344028" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1071 "![](images/0b/0bfc66c5e19ff0fe88cba465c6b045402fc5f8753befa4acb3b1cc3839acf8a7.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1072 #uuid "cd26c621-effc-5c15-8ae1-c10830f17e99" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521548 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521401 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1069 "q-347433" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1071 "Consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=1[x\\ge 2]\\cdot (x-2)^{2}+1[-1\\le x<2]\\cdot 1+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $g(-3)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1072 #uuid "cde00f71-de2d-5ff7-ae71-1ddcfa0a1b1f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521549 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 2]=1[-3\\ge 2]=0$ (since $-3\\ge 2$ is false)\n\n- $1[-1\\le x<2]=1[-1\\le -3<2]=0$ (since $-1\\le -3$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =1[-3\\ge 2]\\cdot (-5)^{2}+1[-1\\le -3<2]\\cdot 1+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ only the third term contributes to the result." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521363 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1069 "q-346669" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=6,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=2,\\,{x}_{5}=-1\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1072 #uuid "ce2c0db9-ad0b-5196-91d2-2f4a888aa062" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521550 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[-3>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[6>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 6+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0+6+0+2+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521360 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1069 "q-351127" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1071 "Let $C=\\{1,3,7,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[4\\in C]?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1072 #uuid "d217e754-13bb-55c1-bf52-1609c0455dd0" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521551 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[4\\in C]$ has $x=4$ and $A=C.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[4\\in C]={1}_{C}(4).\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521349 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1069 "q-344736" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1071 "![](images/bc/bc7e248783ccf6b1eda95a1c2c80300e48d8cdb48f39ea07296ed3bf51bfaa29.png)\n\nA small, circular puddle evaporates on a floor. If the puddle’s area is decreasing at a rate of $32\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ calculate the rate of change of its circumference at the moment when the radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe circumference of the puddle is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1072 #uuid "d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521552 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=8,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=8\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (8)=16\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi $ and $C=16\\pi ,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521444 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1069 "q-41715" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. $Q{Q}^{T}=I$\n\nII. ${Q}^{2}$ is the zero matrix\n\nIII. If $A=QB{Q}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1072 #uuid "d61adfc9-5b12-5408-9a76-aa7eede82be4" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521553 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\nIt's true by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{2}=Q\\ne O.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521348 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521354 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521405 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1069 "q-335606" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.5x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1072 #uuid "d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521554 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521399 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1069 "q-100057" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1071 "![](images/6d/6dd62794ee072530cd2b6c8028101b58ae617d6757d29f79de90d1a439f6529b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1072 #uuid "d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521555 1074 "We find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ So we calculate\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}x-1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(6(1)-1)(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521414 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1069 "q-123550" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{22}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{90})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{94}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{37\\pi }{90})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{76}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{25})$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1072 #uuid "da03fb8c-a7ca-5696-a1f6-3635e0d03dad" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521556 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${22}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{22}^{∘}=22\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${94}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{94}^{∘}=94\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{37\\pi }{90}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${76}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{76}^{∘}=76\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{19\\pi }{25}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521446 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1069 "q-25250" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1071 "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x+4$ by completing the square." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1072 #uuid "db65568c-c0a4-5de2-8240-a716b187636f" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521557 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x-2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x-2) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521375 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1069 "q-344649" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1071 "![](images/cb/cb048e7ac152ffbc740d611726871f395b4c6c4abcc324a4eed890e6116ec814.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually increases the size of a square opening by lengthening its diagonals at a rate of $1.6\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening increasing when each diagonal measures $25\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1072 #uuid "dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521558 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=25.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6\\cdot 25=40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square opening is increasing at a rate of \n$40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521334 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1069 "q-346680" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1071 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=1[x\\ge -1]\\cdot {x}^{2}+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-3)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1072 #uuid "e022df96-ab83-5f54-81a6-300069e7bfc3" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521559 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -1]=1[-3\\ge -1]=0$ (since $-3\\ge -1$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =1[-3\\ge -1]\\cdot (-3)^{2}+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521350 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521422 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521438 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1069 "q-335612" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.05x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1072 #uuid "e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521560 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521342 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1069 "q-2773" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1071 "![](images/fd/fd63afc83917b365d1b91bf4b033be10b64c76a18e7aa16cb8019dc8ef197eb1.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $6\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $4\\text{ cm}$ long?" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1072 #uuid "e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521561 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(6)\\cdot 2(4)=48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521423 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1069 "q-98374" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1071 "Which of the following are natural numbers?\n\nI. $-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $-4$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1072 #uuid "e58a84f8-02f0-5e14-9a24-64165fe53601" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521562 1074 "The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers.\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…\n$$\n\nOf the given numbers, $3$ is a natural number, but $-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $-4$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521344 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1069 "q-67032" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Twenty more than a number.\"" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1072 #uuid "e800ccd8-93c4-50ec-b967-b7f8e3af90fc" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521563 1074 "If we denote our number by $a$, then the phrase \"twenty more than a number\" can be written as\n\n$$\na+20.\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521370 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1069 "q-351130" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1071 "Let $F=\\{3,6,9,15\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[8\\in F]?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1072 #uuid "e8d6c64e-e665-50e4-b8dc-09f085134af3" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521564 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[8\\in F]$ has $x=8$ and $A=F.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[8\\in F]={1}_{F}(8).\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521346 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1069 "q-140409" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1071 "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}-3y-5$ by completing the square." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1072 #uuid "edbc0b13-6a6e-5c66-aaa8-e675f3a4a51e" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521565 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}+3y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+3y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-3y-5 & =-({(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521403 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1069 "q-351129" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1071 "Let $E=\\{0,4,8,12\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[10\\in E]?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1072 #uuid "ee0e20b3-c728-5a30-b95b-8f191603cec6" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521566 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[10\\in E]$ has $x=10$ and $A=E.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[10\\in E]={1}_{E}(10).\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521332 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1069 "q-51403" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1071 "The population growth of a developing rural area was studied for $25$ years. The following table shows the population $p(t),$ in hundreds, recorded $t$ years after $1990.$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $90$ | $98$ | $116$ | $138$ | $178$ | $220$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the population growth rate in the year $2005$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=5.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1072 #uuid "eee2d2d0-57b3-5f3a-92f2-640ee03bafc1" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521567 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{p(t+h)-p(t-h)}{2h}.\n$$\n\n \nWe note that year $2005$ corresponds to $t=15.$ The step size is $h=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{p(15+5)-p(15-5)}{2(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p(20)-p(10)}{2(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{178-116}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{10} \\\\  & =6.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the population growth rate in $2005$ was $620$ people per year." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521366 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1069 "q-78645" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1071 "![](images/18/1822626e3be735821c87aedbebfe29c2ede05a38bc71f9ea75e77f643a885c15.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,D,$ and $E$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(3)?$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1072 #uuid "f2ed610a-45fe-59d0-b6a4-e6d3deb1450a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521568 1074 "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,3).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521339 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1069 "q-123546" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{110}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{18})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{140}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{9})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{170}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{18})$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1072 #uuid "f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521569 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${110}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{110}^{∘}=110\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${140}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{140}^{∘}=140\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${170}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{170}^{∘}=170\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521327 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1069 "q-346673" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=-7,\\,{x}_{3}=8,\\,{x}_{4}=9,\\,{x}_{5}=-2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1072 #uuid "f9506abb-49a9-5357-8f1d-b8154670131b" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521570 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-7>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[8>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-2>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0+0+8+9+0 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521380 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1069 "q-158082" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)=x-10$ and $g(x)=x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1072 #uuid "fe2ad98f-6565-54a7-a35e-058849653046" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521571 1074 "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=x-10$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k \\\\  & =x-10+k \\\\  & =x-10+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-10+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-10+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -10+k & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521572 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521626 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521572 1062 #uuid "057e2df5-62de-5772-a9be-1e31132f5c72" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521572 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134226 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521573 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521627 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521573 1062 #uuid "07d130dd-3700-52b3-9d3d-940cb9caaef0" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521573 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521532 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521574 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521623 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521574 1062 #uuid "0a2c83e4-ab25-57ae-8fa0-093c7c02ee67" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521574 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281589 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521575 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521658 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186521575 1062 #uuid "10d5d17f-5e05-568d-892a-89daf97c2f8d" 13194139535595 